  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nOutside of a loner, elevated storm in north central OH, the\novernight period has seen the pre-frontal convection die off\neast of the region while the MCS remains farther west in central\nIL. This results sets up the region to remain in a clearer sky\nenvironment that awaits the arrival of the pre-frontal trough\n(and eventual sfc cold front) to initiate fairly widespread\nshowers and thunderstorms. Hi-res guidance has generally\ncoalesced around the idea of storm initiation between 2pm-4pm\nover eastern OH with storms spreading east through 8pm\nalong/ahead of the pre-frontal trough before lingering showers\nand low probability thunderstorms full push east of the region\nby the early hours Sunday morning ahead of the surface cold\nfront.\n\nClearer skies allowing for maximization of surface heating\nshould push area SBCAPE values towards 1500-2000 J/kg and\nsteepens low-level lapse rates that serves as the main drivers \nfor the hazardous weather threat. Despite weak shear (around \n20kts) and marginal dCAPE (600-800 J/kg) that should limit the\nhigh-end severe potential, discrete storms that merge into \nmulticellular clusters is enough to create scattered instances\nof damaging winds and large hail. Severe threats peak between\n4pm-8pm before subsiding thereafter as buoyancy wanes and the \nsurface decouples.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nResidual shortwave movement combined with cold advection aloft\nmay trigger isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorm\nSunday afternoon and evening coincident with peak heating.\nStorms coming off Lake Erie toward the I-80 corridor may pose an\nisolated damaging wind threat if surface heating maximizes \nsbCAPE (to 1000 J/kg) while mid-level flow strengthens.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nResidual cool northwest flow on the backside of the upper trough\nmay induce enough lift in conjunction with warm Lake Erie waters\nto create light rain showers near/north of I-80 late Sunday into\nMonday.\n\nOtherwise, high pressure and height rises aloft point to a\nprolonged (relatively speaking, in relation to the previous\nmonth) dry period to start next week. High temperature does\nstart below seasonal averages on Monday before rising back near\naverage by mid week. Long range ensembles currently favor\nThursday as the next day with showers/thunderstorms as the next\nupper trough crosses.",
